        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "617e2515-e6df-59b1-881d-544e9cc378b2",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Grigory Margulis, Leonid Levin, Russell Impagliazzo, Michael Luby\n- Concepts: Margulis lemma, leftover hash lemma\n- Topics: differential geometry, cryptography, ergodic theory, diophantine approximation, lattices in Lie groups, discrete subgroups of isometries, non-positively curved Riemannian manifolds\n\nFacts:\n- Grigory Margulis was born on February 24, 1946.\n- Grigory Margulis is a Russian-American mathematician known for work on lattices in Lie groups and for introducing methods from ergodic theory into diophantine approximation.\n- Leonid Levin was born on November 2, 1948.\n- Leonid Levin is a Soviet-American computer scientist.\n- The Margulis lemma is named after Grigory Margulis.\n- The Margulis lemma is a result in differential geometry about discrete subgroups of isometries of non-positively curved Riemannian manifolds.\n- The leftover hash lemma is a lemma in cryptography.\n- Russell Impagliazzo, Leonid Levin, and Michael Luby first stated the leftover hash lemma.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
